Z||000000||Hank is Here||09-20-2001||11:23 PM||||I was trying to think of all the current GM bolt patterns <P>RWD:<BR>-Big Block <BR>-Small Block <BR>-V6/4cyl (2.5, 2.8, 3.4, 3.8)<P>FWD:  <BR>-V6/4cyl (same pattern as above 2.5, 2.8, 3.1, 3.3, 3.4, 3.8, N*) don't know a name for this pattern.<BR>-4cyl (quad 4 based pattern)  <P>Unknowns: (to me at least) <BR>-New trailblazer pattern<BR>-Opel FWD and RWD V6 (Saturn and Caddies)<BR>-Newer 2.2 in the 2wd S10's (I assume it is a V6/4cyl pattern)<BR>-Duramax diesel pattern in the full size pickups <P>What CURRENT patterns am I missing?  <BR>Just trying to think GM here for a while.  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Raydar||09-20-2001||11:39 PM||||Don't know if this will be useful or not...<BR>RWD Chevy V-8s all use the same pattern, I believe. Big block, small block, LT1, LT4 and (I  think) LS1. Not sure about the LT5 (ZR1 Vette motor) though.<BR>The 4.3 also shares this pattern.<P>Other GM V-8s used the B-O-P (Buick, Olds, Pontiac) pattern. 70's and early 80's 3.8s also shared this pattern.<P>The 4.9 uses pretty much the same pattern as the 2.5, 2.8 and 3800. The Northstar uses a pattern similar to this, but the starter is in the center of the "V", so the bellhousing has to be modded, slightly. <P>The 4 cylinder GM "marine" engine uses a Chevy V-8 tranny bolt pattern, I believe. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
